+<main>
+
+  <section class="card">
+    <h2>Most of this list is decided in your first two years.</h2>
+    <p class="lede">That is the difference between a graduate fellowship calendar and a postdoctoral
+      one. The largest award here can be applied to exactly once, in the first year of the PhD, with a
+      deadline that falls about two months after arriving. Several others close their windows at year
+      two or year three. A student who first looks at this page in year four is reading a much shorter
+      list than a student who looks in the summer before matriculating. Each entry carries the
+      eligibility window, the money, the citizenship rule and the next deadline &mdash; computed live in your
+      browser from the recurring pattern, so the dates stay right after this page stops being edited.
+      Terms were verified against primary sources on 22 September 2026.</p>
+    <div class="stats">
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Fellowships tracked</div><div class="v" id="nTotal">&mdash;</div>
+        <div class="s">predoctoral, annually recurring</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Open to visa holders</div><div class="v" id="nVisa">&mdash;</div>
+        <div class="s">no citizenship or residency bar</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Closing in 90 days</div><div class="v" id="nSoon">&mdash;</div>
+        <div class="s">counted live in your browser</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Need a nomination</div><div class="v" id="nNom">&mdash;</div>
+        <div class="s">someone else has to submit</div></div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="foot">
+      <p><strong>Looking for something to propose?</strong> The companion
+        <a href="grad-project-ideas.html">project ideas</a> page turns this list around: research themes
+        drawn from the departmental lab profiles, with project directions sized for a thesis aim and a
+        fellowship application, each tagged with the awards here that fit it.</p>
+      <p class="gate"><strong>Start with the citizenship filter, then the year-in-programme rule.</strong>
+        Every federal mechanism on this page &mdash; the F31, the F30, the F99/K00, the NSF GRFP, NDSEG, the
+        two DOE programmes &mdash; is closed to students on a visa. What is left for an international student
+        is real and worth knowing precisely: the Gilliam is the largest of them, and the disease
+        foundations are markedly more open than the government is.</p>
+      <p class="flag"><strong>Two eligibility facts that cost people a cycle.</strong> The NSF GRFP no
+        longer accepts second-year graduate students, so an enrolled PhD student realistically gets one
+        attempt, in year one &mdash; and MD/PhD students are excluded from it outright. Separately, GRFP's
+        Life Sciences scope now excludes patient-oriented, clinical, epidemiological and
+        disease- or treatment-focused research, which disqualifies a large share of biomedical projects
+        no matter what year the applicant is in. Read the excluded-subject list before the eligibility
+        rules.</p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
