+
+<main>
+
+  <section class="card">
+    <h2>Thirty-six directions, none of them assignments</h2>
+    <p class="lede">A thesis has to be the student's own, and so does a fellowship application &mdash; reviewers
+      can tell when a proposal was handed down, and the F31 is scored in large part on whether the work
+      will train the person writing it. What follows is deliberately upstream of both: research themes
+      represented in this department, the techniques already running in the building, and directions
+      that sit adjacent to existing strengths rather than inside them. Find the theme your rotation
+      touched, then argue with the ideas under it. The version you disagree your way into will be better
+      than anything here.</p>
+    <div class="stats">
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Research themes</div><div class="v">12</div>
+        <div class="s">drawn from 20 active labs</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Project directions</div><div class="v">36</div>
+        <div class="s">three per theme</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Cross-lab ideas</div><div class="v">6</div>
+        <div class="s">needing two groups to work</div></div>
+      <div class="stat"><div class="k">Fellowships mapped</div><div class="v">21</div>
+        <div class="s">see the companion page</div></div>
+    </div>
+    <div class="foot">
+      <p class="gate"><strong>Read this before you pick a direction, if you are in your first year.</strong>
+        The NSF GRFP is the one award here that a first-year can win and no one else can, and it now
+        excludes patient-oriented, clinical, epidemiological, and disease- or treatment-focused research
+        from its Life Sciences scope. That is not a formality &mdash; it means the same laboratory can host a
+        GRFP-eligible mechanistic project and a GRFP-ineligible translational one. Directions below that
+        can be framed for the GRFP are tagged for it; the ones that cannot are not, and the omission is
+        deliberate rather than an oversight.</p>
+      <p><strong>How the fellowship tags work.</strong> Each direction carries the awards whose scope and
+        eligibility actually fit it &mdash; not every award a graduate student could theoretically enter.
+        Deadlines, citizenship rules and year-in-programme windows for all of them are on the
+        <a href="grad-fellowships.html">graduate fellowships page</a>. Where a direction is tagged
+        <em>NIH F30</em> rather than <em>F31</em>, it is one that reads particularly well from a dual-degree
+        student; the F31 is available either way.</p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <div class="filters">
+    <button class="fchip" data-f="cl" data-v="all" aria-pressed="true">All themes</button>
+    <button class="fchip" data-f="cl" data-v="virus" aria-pressed="false">Virology &amp; RNA biology</button>
