+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"Is proteostasis the point of contact between metabolism and oxidative stress?",
+   p:"Degradation machinery and central metabolic enzymes are linked in this organism, but whether the degradation system is reading the metabolic state or setting it is unresolved. Perturbing each independently, with metabolite measurement as the readout, would settle the direction of the arrow.",
+   w:"The direction-of-causality framing is what separates a descriptive aim from a mechanistic one, and reviewers of predoctoral applications are specifically trained to look for it.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","NIH F99/K00"]},
+  {t:"Does the host impose the redox conditions the system evolved for?",
+   p:"Redox-regulated chaperones are studied under defined conditions in vitro. Measuring the actual redox environment the bacterium experiences inside a macrophage, and then reproducing it, would test whether the in vitro conditions everyone uses are the relevant ones.",
+   w:"A methods-development aim with a clear biological payoff, and one that puts a student in contact with the myeloid biology running elsewhere in the department.",
+   f:["NIH F31","NSF GRFP","HHMI Gilliam"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"amr", cl:"bact", name:"Colonisation, within-host adaptation and antimicrobial resistance",
+ hereIf:"You are interested in why a carried organism becomes an infection, in isolate genomics, or in resistance as an evolutionary process rather than a marker.",
+ caps:"Clinical isolate collections and population genetics, whole-genome sequencing and comparative genomics, quorum sensing and virulence assays, colonisation models, machine learning for resistance and virulence prediction, neonatal and paediatric infection models.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"Which within-host mutations are adaptive and which are hitchhiking?",
+   p:"Longitudinal isolates from a single carrier accumulate changes, and the ones that recur across patients are usually called adaptive. Reconstructing candidate mutations individually in a clean background and competing them in a colonisation model would test that inference directly rather than inferring it from parallelism.",
+   w:"Takes a genomics observation the lab already owns and turns it into experimental genetics &mdash; which is exactly the pivot that makes a computational student's training plan credible.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam","Google PhD Fellowship"]},
+  {t:"Does resistance acquisition cost anything the host can exploit?",
+   p:"Fitness costs of resistance are usually measured in broth. Measuring them during colonisation, in the presence of an immune response, asks whether resistant lineages are vulnerable in a way that could be targeted without a new antibiotic.",
+   w:"A therapeutically suggestive result from an entirely mechanistic experiment. Frame the aims around the evolutionary question and the translational reading follows without the proposal becoming treatment-focused.",
+   f:["NIH F31","PhRMA Drug Discovery","NSF GRFP"]},
+  {t:"Is neonatal colonisation a different process or the same one earlier?",
+   p:"Colonisation in the first weeks of life happens in a host with a different immune repertoire, a different microbiota and a different epithelium. Whether the determinants that matter in adults matter at all in neonates is testable with the same isolates and the same models, run in parallel.",
+   w:"A comparison rather than a new system, which keeps the project finishable, and it connects to the early-life microbiome work already here.",
+   f:["NIH F31","NIH F30","CF Foundation Traineeship","AHA Predoctoral"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"malaria", cl:"bact", name:"Parasite pathogenesis and host vascular biology",
+ hereIf:"You want to work on how a parasite damages tissue rather than on the parasite itself &mdash; vascular biology, mitochondrial stress, or biomarkers with a mechanism behind them.",
+ caps:"<em>Plasmodium</em> culture and infection models, blood&ndash;brain barrier and endothelial assays, mitochondrial stress and oxidative-damage measurement, purinergic and farnesylation pathway pharmacology, RNA-seq and vascular profiling, biomarker discovery.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"Is barrier disruption driven by the parasite or by the host's response to its debris?",
+   p:"Parasite-derived material and the host inflammatory response both correlate with barrier failure. Applying purified parasite products to endothelium in the absence of immune cells, and then adding them back one population at a time, separates two mechanisms that are confounded in every animal model.",
+   w:"A reductionist design aimed at a question normally addressed only in vivo, and the endothelial work is learnable inside one year.",
+   f:["NIH F31","AHA Predoctoral","NSF GRFP"]},
+  {t:"Does mitochondrial stress in endothelium explain the severity gradient?",
+   p:"Lipid-mediated mitochondrial stress is an established mechanism and severity is an established clinical gradient, but the two have not been linked quantitatively. Measuring mitochondrial function in the vessels of animals across that gradient would test whether it is the variable that tracks outcome.",
+   w:"Quantitative, mechanistic, and it produces the dose&ndash;response relationship that any downstream intervention argument depends on.",
+   f:["NIH F31","AHA Predoctoral","NIH F30"]},
+  {t:"Can protection be uncoupled from parasite clearance?",
+   p:"If the damage is host-side, then blocking the damage pathway should protect without changing parasite burden. That is a sharp, falsifiable prediction, and the pharmacology to test it &mdash; purinergic and farnesylation inhibitors &mdash; is already in use in the lab.",
+   w:"A single decisive experiment at the centre of the thesis, with a clear negative result that is still publishable. Committees like proposals that can fail informatively.",
+   f:["NIH F31","AHA Predoctoral","PhRMA Translational Medicine","Fogarty LAUNCH"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"myeloid", cl:"immune", name:"Tissue myeloid cells and the immunology of respiratory infection",
+ hereIf:"You want to work on macrophage subsets in tissue, on why inflammation resolves, or on immunity in the lung specifically rather than in the spleen.",
+ caps:"Tissue myeloid subset identification and sorting, subset-specific depletion and conditional genetics, respiratory virus and systemic bacterial infection models, type 2 immunity and fibrosis models, tumour-associated macrophage work, nanoparticle reprogramming, imaging.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"Which subset ends the inflammation, and is that the same one that started it?",
+   p:"Resolution is usually attributed to a change of state in the same cells that drove inflammation. Whether it is instead a distinct, later-recruited subset is answerable by depleting each population at defined times after infection rather than at the start.",
+   w:"Timed depletion is a simple idea that almost nobody does, because it requires committing to a time course. That makes it a good thesis: unglamorous, decidable, and the result is the same either way.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"Do nerve-associated macrophages sense the pathogen or the tissue damage?",
+   p:"Anatomically defined myeloid subsets sit where they could be responding to either. Separating the two requires a sterile injury that mimics the damage without the pathogen, run alongside an infection that is controlled before damage occurs.",
+   w:"A comparison of two perturbations rather than a new model system, which is how a student gets an answer inside a PhD rather than a description.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"Is the tumour macrophage programme a corrupted resolution programme?",
+   p:"Tumour-associated macrophages and resolving macrophages share markers and are usually described independently. Profiling both in the same animal, and testing whether a reprogramming strategy developed for one works on the other, would ask whether they are the same state reached by different routes.",
+   w:"Connects infection immunology to tumour immunology using techniques the lab runs for both, which gives a student two committee constituencies and two funding routes.",
+   f:["NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam","NIH F99/K00"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"vaccine", cl:"immune", name:"Human vaccine immunology and the durability of antibody responses",
+ hereIf:"You are drawn to human samples, systems immunology, or the question of why two people given the same vaccine end up in different places a year later.",
+ caps:"Human vaccination and infection cohorts, Tfh and CD4 T cell analysis, multiplex and avidity immunoassays, single-cell and spatial multi-omics, comparative vaccine- versus infection-induced immunity, immune-imprinting analysis, viral evolution and escape mapping.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"Is imprinting a property of the repertoire or of the germinal centre?",
+   p:"Prior exposure constrains the response to a new antigen, but whether the constraint lies in which B cells exist or in which ones are allowed to enter and persist in the germinal centre is unresolved. Paired repertoire and Tfh analysis in the same individuals across a vaccination series can separate the two.",
+   w:"Human, mechanistic and answerable with existing cohorts &mdash; and the analysis skills it teaches are the ones that make a graduate transferable to any immunology lab afterwards.",
+   f:["NIH F31","NIH F30","AHA Predoctoral"]},
+  {t:"Does avidity maturation continue after the antibody titre stops changing?",
+   p:"Durability is almost always reported as titre. If avidity keeps improving while titre plateaus, then the standard measure is missing the part of the response that matters for protection &mdash; a possibility that is directly measurable in banked longitudinal samples.",
+   w:"A measurement-reframing project: low risk, high generality, and it produces a methods paper plus a biology paper from the same dataset.",
+   f:["NIH F31","NIH F30","AHA Predoctoral"]},
+  {t:"Can escape be predicted from the shape of the response rather than the sequence?",
+   p:"Escape prediction is normally a sequence problem. Asking instead whether the breadth and focus of an individual's response predicts which variants will escape it turns a virology question into an immunology one, with the same cohorts on both sides.",
+   w:"A genuinely interdisciplinary aim, and the computational half is substantial enough to justify a quantitative training plan and the awards that go with one.",
+   f:["NIH F31","Google PhD Fellowship","NIH F99/K00"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"microbiome", cl:"comp", name:"Microbiome ecology, early life, and learning from longitudinal data",
+ hereIf:"You came in with mathematics, statistics or computer science and want a biological question, or you have microbiome data and suspect the standard analysis is throwing most of it away.",
+ caps:"Metagenomics and multi-omics integration, ecological and dynamical-systems modelling, machine learning and network inference, tensor factorisation and dimensionality reduction, longitudinal and time-series modelling, milk and infant cohort data, phage&ndash;bacterium interaction analysis.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"Does the order of colonisation matter as much as the membership?",
+   p:"Community composition is modelled as a state; arrival order is rarely modelled at all. Testing whether sequence of colonisation predicts the eventual community better than membership does, in cohorts where sampling is dense enough to see it, asks whether ecology's priority-effect literature applies to the infant gut.",
+   w:"A theory-driven question with an existing dataset, which is the fastest route from a first-year rotation to a fellowship aim. Entirely basic in framing, so the GRFP stays open.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","Google PhD Fellowship","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"What does milk actually select for?",
+   p:"Milk components correlate with infant community structure, but correlation across mother&ndash;infant pairs cannot distinguish selection from inheritance. Source-tracking combined with in vitro selection on defined milk fractions would separate what is transmitted from what is favoured.",
+   w:"Combines a computational method with a small, tractable wet-lab component &mdash; the standard way a quantitative student builds a training plan that a biology study section will fund.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"Can phage dynamics be predicted well enough to be used?",
+   p:"Phage&ndash;bacterium dynamics are usually described retrospectively. Building a model that forecasts them in a held-out time series, and then testing the forecast against a manipulated community, is the difference between fitting and predicting.",
+   w:"Prediction with a held-out test is a standard a methods-oriented committee will respect, and a failed forecast is itself a finding. Strong fit for the computational awards as well as the F31.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","Google PhD Fellowship","DOE CSGF","NIH F31"]}
+ ]},
+
+{id:"structure", cl:"struct", name:"Structural biology of membrane transport in infection and immunity",
+ hereIf:"You want to see the mechanism rather than infer it, and you are willing to spend two years learning to before you get an answer.",
+ caps:"Cryo-EM of membrane proteins, biophysical analysis of transport systems, antigen-processing transporter biochemistry, bacterial ABC transporter reconstitution, substrate-recognition and conformational-dynamics assays, protein purification at scale.",
+ ideas:[
+  {t:"How does an antigen transporter decide what to move?",
+   p:"Substrate selectivity in antigen processing is described in terms of preferences rather than structures. Capturing the transporter with different substrates bound, and relating occupancy to transport rate, would convert a statistical preference into a physical mechanism with consequences for which epitopes are ever presented.",
+   w:"Immunology that is genuinely structural, which means it can be sponsored from either side and gives the student a defensible claim to both fields at the end.",
+   f:["NIH F31","NSF GRFP","HHMI Gilliam"]},
+  {t:"Do bacterial secretion transporters share a conformational cycle with their host counterparts?",
+   p:"Structurally related transporters on both sides of an infection are usually studied by different communities. Solving them under matched conditions and comparing the cycles directly asks whether the similarity is ancestral or convergent &mdash; and whether an inhibitor of one would touch the other.",
+   w:"A comparative structural project with a built-in specificity question, which is the form of the argument any antimicrobial development effort eventually has to make.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","PhRMA Drug Discovery"]},
+  {t:"What does the energy-coupling step actually cost?",
+   p:"Transport stoichiometry is often assumed from homology. Measuring it directly, for a transporter where the substrate matters for virulence, tests whether the system is operating near its thermodynamic limit &mdash; which would explain why it is so tightly regulated.",
+   w:"Quantitative biophysics with an infection-biology motivation. It is a small, complete question, and small complete questions are what get finished before a thesis committee loses patience.",
+   f:["NSF GRFP","NIH F31","Hertz","DOE CSGF"]}
+ ]}
+
+];
